WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Secure design starts with choosing the right tool for the workflow.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.640
SCP is often used for straightforward file copying.

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:15.500
SFTP-style workflows may be preferred when a controlled file-transfer interface is the right operational model.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.280
rsync is useful when synchronization, comparison, and efficient incremental transfer matter.

5
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:28.680
A single-file copy, a directory tree sync, a release artifact promotion, and a backup mirror are different jobs.

6
00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:37.580
They may have different requirements for metadata preservation, bandwidth efficiency, repeatability, partial-transfer behavior, delete behavior, and recovery.

7
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.400
Choosing by habit can hide those differences.

8
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:47.320
rsync is powerful because it can compare source and destination state and transfer only what changed.

9
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.380
That is useful, but synchronization is more than copying.

10
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:58.560
It may preserve or change metadata, skip or include files according to rules, and optionally remove data from the destination.

11
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.540
Those behaviors need review before production use.

12
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.540
SCP can be simpler for a known file or small controlled set of files.

13
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.240
Simplicity can be a security advantage when the workflow is truly simple.

14
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:18.660
But even simple copy workflows still need host verification, dedicated identities, path validation, and result checking.

15
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:23.260
The defensive habit is to write down what the transfer must accomplish.

16
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:30.160
Are you copying, synchronizing, preserving attributes, replacing an existing release, or collecting evidence?

17
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:38.420
Once the workflow is clear, choose the simplest safe tool and verify its behavior instead of assuming all transfer commands are interchangeable.
